<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-detection-reserve> {
  ex:l5-v03a-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-concentration-dependent-detection ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-v03a-heading, ex:l5-v03a-plot, ex:l5-v03a-math ; cd:narration "V03a, optional reserve. Fix the concentration at 2 mg/L and specify a positive-finding probability of one half for this new teaching model. Ten independent repeat decisions under stable conditions give a binomial positive count. The bars are exact count probabilities, not observations or a simulation. The expected count is five, but no realized series is forced to contain exactly five positives; every count from zero through ten is possible. Near a decision region, the same stable sample can produce both positive and negative findings because its analytical signal varies. The binomial model requires a common probability at that concentration and independence of the repeat errors. Drift, shared preparation effects, depletion or a changing concentration can invalidate those assumptions. Ten repeat decisions do not become ten independently selected environmental samples. This concentration-specific probability is distinct from V02's target-population prevalence and marginal positive-finding probability. No physical or validated method-performance claim follows from the chosen one-half probability."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-v03b-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-concentration-dependent-detection ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-v03b-heading, ex:l5-v03b-plot, ex:l5-v03b-math ; cd:narration "V03b, optional reserve. One possible concentration-response family models natural log odds as an intercept plus slope times concentration. Odds divide positive by negative probability; the logarithm acts on a dimensionless ratio. For this illustration choose intercept minus four and slope two litres per milligram. Concentration uses mg/L, so the slope times concentration is dimensionless. Subscripts zero and one label parameters, not powers. The curve gives positive-finding probabilities about 0.119 at 1 mg/L, exactly 0.5 at 2 and about 0.881 at 3. The one-half midpoint matches the preceding binomial view. It is not automatically an analytical detection limit or a signal decision threshold. At zero concentration the illustrative probability is about 0.018; these chosen parameters are not a fit to V02's false-positive rate of 0.05 or any calibration observations. The positive slope makes this chosen curve rise, but a logistic family itself is a modelling choice, not a natural law. The displayed 0 to 4 mg/L window is not a validated operating range. Changing concentration units, centering or coding requires changing the coefficients accordingly. Sampled curve coordinates are joined approximately and are not raw measurements."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-v03c-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-concentration-dependent-detection ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-v03c-heading, ex:l5-v03c-list ; cd:narration "V03c, optional reserve. Before fitting or transferring a detection curve, define the statistical unit and what is being repeated: signals from one preparation, independently prepared aliquots or separately selected environmental samples. These designs do not justify the same independence claim. Specify one target analyte, the decision rule, concentration units or coding, matrix, preparation and experimental conditions. Reference concentrations and repeat classifications across a justified range are needed to assess a fitted model; estimated coefficients and predicted probabilities carry uncertainty. A drawn sigmoid cannot demonstrate the fit, uncertainty, absence of drift or transferability to another matrix. Logistic models with intercept and slope need not accommodate all real detection behavior; suitable models may require different asymptotes or other structure. Do not extrapolate a plotted curve into an unvalidated range, declare its midpoint a detection limit or infer population prevalence from it. These three views illustrate a model family only, not analytical validation or a regulatory decision. The main lecture remains separate from this optional reserve."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-v03a-heading a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-v03a-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-v03a-plot a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-v03a-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-v03a-math a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-v03a-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-v03b-heading a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:l5-v03b-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
